drills.solution:
export function slugify(title, maxLength = 60) {
  const slug = title
    .normalize('NFD')
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');
  return slug.slice(0, maxLength).replace(/-+$/, '');
}

tests.code:
const s = mod.slugify('aaaa bbbb cccc', 10);
assert.equal(s, 'aaaa-bbbb');
assert.ok(s.length <= 10);
assert.equal(mod.slugify('x'.repeat(100)).length, 60);

drills.explanation:
Normalise first (so accents become separate marks you can delete), then lowercase, then collapse, then trim; cap the length LAST and re-trim:

```js
const slug = title.normalize('NFD').replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')
  .toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');
return slug.slice(0, maxLength).replace(/-+$/, '');
```

Lowercasing before normalising works too; collapsing BEFORE removing accents does not, because the accented letter is not in `a-z` and becomes a dash, so 'Crème' turns into 'cr-me' and the test "accents, punctuation and spaces" fails. Cutting to `maxLength` without the final re-trim leaves 'aaaa-bbbb-', and "long titles are capped without a trailing dash" fails. Replacing single characters instead of runs (no `+` in the class) produces double dashes and fails "runs collapse to one dash, ends are trimmed".

